            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Measures how unpredictable something is from how often each of its possible outcomes occurs: 0 when one outcome is certain, largest when every outcome is equally likely. In bits, it is how many yes/no questions it takes on average to guess the outcome: a fair coin is 1 bit, a fair four-way choice 2.\n\nUse it to measure variety: how evenly a performer uses the space (counts per zone from np.histogram2d), how varied the gestures in a phrase are (counts per cluster from scipy.kmeans and np.bincount), how predictable a sequence of states is, how spread a distribution of directions is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.entropy\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ncounts:\nHow often each outcome occurs (or each one's probability): a list of numbers 0 or more. They need not add up to anything; they are scaled to total 1. Every input is measured and sent.\n\nunits:\nAn option, under the node: bits (base 2, the default), natural (base e) or digits (base 10).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nentropy:\nThe unpredictability.\n\nevenness:\nThe entropy as a fraction of the most possible for that many outcomes: 1 when all are equally likely, 0 when one is certain - comparable between lists of different lengths. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA fair coin (5 5): 1 bit. A coin that lands heads 9 times in 10 (9 1): 0.47 bits - much more predictable. Four equally likely outcomes (1 1 1 1): 2 bits. A metro re-sends the counts twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.histogram, np.bincount and np.unique count outcomes. scipy.circstd measures the spread of angles.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Measures how unpredictable something is from how often each of its possible outcomes occurs: 0 when one outcome is certain, largest when every outcome is equally likely. In bits, it is how many yes/no questions it takes on average to guess the outcome: a fair coin is 1 bit, a fair four-way choice 2.\n\nUse it to measure variety: how evenly a performer uses the space (counts per zone from np.histogram2d), how varied the gestures in a phrase are (counts per cluster from scipy.kmeans and np.bincount), how predictable a sequence of states is, how spread a distribution of directions is.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.entropy\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ncounts:\nHow often each outcome occurs (or each one's probability): a list of numbers 0 or more. They need not add up to anything; they are scaled to total 1. Every input is measured and sent.\n\nunits:\nAn option, under the node: bits (base 2, the default), natural (base e) or digits (base 10).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nentropy:\nThe unpredictability.\n\nevenness:\nThe entropy as a fraction of the most possible for that many outcomes: 1 when all are equally likely, 0 when one is certain - comparable between lists of different lengths. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA fair coin (5 5): 1 bit. A coin that lands heads 9 times in 10 (9 1): 0.47 bits - much more predictable. Four equally likely outcomes (1 1 1 1): 2 bits. A metro re-sends the counts twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.histogram, np.bincount and np.unique count outcomes. scipy.circstd measures the spread of angles."
        },
